\label{def:ubq}
    Let $X$ be a geodesic metric space. A connected subspace $Z \subset X$ is called \emph{narrow} if it is quasi-isometric to a subset of $\mathbf{R}$, with respect to the ambient metric. If $Z$ is not narrow then it is said to be \emph{wide}. We define the \emph{uniformly bisecting quasi-geodesics} property, denoted (\ref{eq:BQ}), as follows:
        \begin{equation}
  \tag{$\rm{UBQ}$}\label{eq:BQ}
  \parbox{4in}{    \strut
    There exists $\sigma > 0$ such that for any connected, bi-infinite quasi-geodesic $\rho \subset X$, we have that $X \setminus N_\sigma(\rho)$ contains exactly two wide connected components. 
    \strut
  }
\end{equation}
We call $\sigma$ the \emph{separation constant}.

